\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{The complete three-layer cube}

\p{The all-size law has two reasons to be followed by a separate
three-layer account. A #{3\times3\times3} cube has no moving centers or
coupled edges, and its familiar three tests can be checked directly on the
six outer-face generators. We use a fixed frame and unmarked centers. A
labelled state is therefore}
##{(\sigma,x;\tau,z)\in S_8\times(\mathbb Z_3)^8
      \times S_{12}\times(\mathbb Z_2)^{12}.}
\p{Here #{\sigma} and #{\tau} record corner and edge positions. The corner
frame uses the U/D sticker to record a twist in #{\mathbb Z_3}; an edge frame
uses the U/D or F/B sticker convention to record a flip in #{\mathbb Z_2}.
The six centers fix the frame but have no recorded orientation. This is the
ordinary cube model, rather than a supercube model with marked centers.}

\subtree{
  \title{Generators and the three obstructions}

  \p{Write #{R,L,U,D,F,B} for clockwise quarter-turns as seen from the
  corresponding face; inverses and squares are allowed. Each turn is a
  four-cycle on corners and a four-cycle on edges. Thus it changes both
  permutation signs, leaving}
  ##{\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)=\operatorname{sgn}(\tau)}
  \p{unchanged. A turn of U or D changes no corner twist in this convention;
  a turn of another face adds one and subtracts one at opposite corners. The
  four affected edge frames change in pairs. Consequently every reachable
  state satisfies}
  ##{\sum_{i=1}^{8}x_i=0\pmod3,\qquad
      \sum_{j=1}^{12}z_j=0\pmod2.}

  \p{These checks are generator checks, so they also cover inverses, powers
  and arbitrary words. They give immediate impossible states: one twisted
  corner has corner sum #{1}; one flipped edge has edge sum #{1}; and a swap
  of two corners alone has odd corner sign but even edge sign. A state can
  pass all three tests and still require a construction; necessity is not
  sufficiency. The coordinate convention and invariant calculation agree with the 3-layer
  case of \citet{Theorems 8--18}{salkinder2021nxnxn}.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Localized effects and sufficiency}

  \p{We now describe a constructive cleanup. Let
  #{[a,b]=aba^{-1}b^{-1}} and #{[a:b]=aba^{-1}}. The word}
  ##{c=[[R:U],D]}
  \p{is a three-cycle on corners and fixes the other corner positions. A
  direct coordinate replay gives this cycle; conjugating by face turns moves
  it through the connected corner orbit. Hence these conjugates generate
  #{A_8}. For edges, the outer-turn word}
  ##{e=R\,U^{-1}\,R\,U\,R\,U\,R\,U^{-1}\,R^{-1}\,U^{-1}\,R^2}
  \p{is a three-cycle on three edges and fixes the corners (the familiar
  U-permutation). Conjugates generate #{A_{12}}. The displayed words are
  useful witnesses, not a claim that they are short in every metric.}

  \p{Given a state satisfying the sign equation, apply one face turn when
  the common sign is odd. The remaining corner and edge permutations are
  even. Use the conjugated corner cycles to clear #{\sigma}, then the
  conjugated edge cycles to clear #{\tau}; each cleanup is chosen in the
  subgroup that fixes the already-cleared piece type. The sign equation is
  exactly what makes both cleanups possible.}

  \p{After positions are restored, the orientation kernels are generated by
  local commutators. The standard corner trigger
  #{(R^{-1}D^{-1}RD)^2} restores positions while twisting two corners in
  opposite directions; setup, trigger, and inverse setup transport this
  effect to any pair. For edges, the local trigger
  #{F R U R^{-1}U^{-1}F^{-1}} is combined with its conjugate and inverse
  setup to give a pure two-edge flip. A direct sticker replay checks that
  support and the two flipped signs. Their transported effects generate the kernels}
  ##{\ker((\mathbb Z_3)^8\to\mathbb Z_3)\quad\text{and}\quad
      \ker((\mathbb Z_2)^{12}\to\mathbb Z_2).}
  \p{The two displayed sums are therefore the only orientation obstructions.
  Reversing the cleanup word reaches every tuple satisfying the three tests.
  This is a constructive subgroup argument; it does not identify a shortest
  solution.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Count and a worked reachability check}

  \p{There are #{8!} corner permutations, #{12!} edge permutations,
  #{3^8} raw corner orientations and #{2^{12}} raw edge orientations. The
  sign equation removes a factor of two, and the two orientation sums remove
  factors of three and two. Thus the number of labelled reachable states is}
  ##{8!\,12!\,3^7\,2^{10}
      =43\,252\,003\,274\,489\,856\,000.}
  \p{For a concrete reachability check, start from solved and apply the
  corner cycle #{c}. Its corner permutation is a 3-cycle, its edge
  permutation is the identity, and its orientation sums remain zero, so it
  passes every test. Applying #{c^{-1}} returns to solved. More generally,
  the cleanup above factors any passing state into a sign correction, even
  corner and edge permutations, and orientation-pair operations; this gives
  an explicit solve word once the required cycles and setup positions have
  been selected.}

  \p{Unmarked centers explain why a whole-cube rotation is not counted as a
  generator and why the center colors fix a frame rather than add twelve
  more coordinates. The all-#{N} chapter keeps these choices visible when
  moving centers and coupled edges return. The next chapters can now discuss
  human methods and computer search using this completely specified 3-layer
  action.}
}
